# Appendix D: the terminal version.
.doc Strata Reference: The Terminal Version
.chapter D
.h1 The Terminal Version [appterm]
.ix Appendix D
.ix Terminal version
.ix Terminal
.ix Text terminal
This appendix describes the terminal version of the Editor and of the
Document Manager: what the terminal must be able to do, how the screen looks
in it, how big it is, which keys a terminal sends and how the keys that Strata
uses for its own commands are reached in other ways.  The terminal version is
the
one that runs in a terminal window, over a remote login and in a multiplexer.
The X11 version, which opens a window of its own, is
described in {n:appx11}; {x:view-screen-kinds} gives
the list of the differences that matter in daily work.

The sections follow the order in which a problem between the keyboard and the
screen shows itself: what the terminal must provide ({n:appterm-needs},
{n:appterm-term}); how the screen looks ({n:appterm-colour},
{n:appterm-chars}, {n:appterm-size}); the keys, which vary most from terminal
to terminal ({n:appterm-esc}, {n:appterm-ctrl}, {n:appterm-reach}); a remote
login ({n:appterm-links}); and last
the differences from the X11 version, how Strata hands the terminal over, and
a table of problems ({n:appterm-diff}, {n:appterm-leave},
{n:appterm-trouble}).  The two versions offer the same commands, so the
appendix has few commands and many keys.

.h2 What the terminal version needs [appterm-needs]
.ix Terminal, requirements
.ix Standard input and output
.ix Interactive terminal
.ix UTF-8
The terminal version needs three things.
.table 16 44
Need | Detail
A terminal on input | The keys must come from a terminal.  A program that
  | reads its keys from a file or from another program cannot be driven.
A terminal on output | The screen must be a terminal too.  The standard
  | error may go elsewhere.
A terminal that can draw | The screen is written in UTF-8 when the terminal reads
  | it, and otherwise with the line characters of the VT100 set, which
  | nearly every terminal has ({n:appterm-charset}).  The keys are read as
  | UTF-8 when they are not plain ASCII ({n:appterm-chars}).  The terminal
  | must support the usual control sequences of the {c:xterm} family, as the
  | terminal emulators of desktops do.
.end
When the keys or the screen are not a terminal, the program writes one line on
the standard error and ends.  The line of the Editor reads {c:needs an
interactive terminal (or an X display with the x11 build)}; the line of the
Document Manager reads the same, with the name of its program in front, and the
line of the Preview that the Editor makes for a document reads {c:Preview needs
an interactive terminal} and then the same words in brackets.  Nothing else is
written, and no file is touched.
.note A terminal version never switches to a window: it does not look at
  the environment variable {c:DISPLAY}.  Likewise the X11 version never falls back
  to a terminal: when it cannot open its display it writes the same line and
  ends.

.h2 Terminals and the environment variable TERM [appterm-term]
.ix TERM
.ix terminfo
.ix Alternate screen
.ix Title of the terminal
The terminal version does not use the terminal descriptions of the system.  It
reads the environment variable {c:TERM} only for two things: how many colors
the terminal shows ({n:appterm-colour}) and whether the terminal is a
multiplexer such as tmux ({n:appterm-charset}).  It runs in any
terminal that understands the usual sequences, whatever {c:TERM} holds; a wrong
{c:TERM} changes the number of colors and nothing else.  When it
starts, Strata switches to
the alternate screen of the terminal, so that the text of the shell comes back
at the end.  After a change of the size of the terminal the whole
screen is drawn again.  Strata never rings the bell.

The terminal is put in raw mode while Strata runs: no echo, no line editing,
and no signals from the keyboard.  Three consequences matter
to the user.
- {k:Ctrl-S} and {k:Ctrl-Q} are keys of Strata (Save and Quit), not the flow
  control of the terminal; the terminal does not stop when you press them.
- {k:Ctrl-C} is Spell Word; it does not interrupt the program, and
  {k:Ctrl-Z} does not suspend it.  Strata cannot be put into the background
  with the keyboard.
- When the program ends in any way, the terminal is given back as it was
  found: the attributes are reset, the cursor is shown, the alternate screen
  is left and the settings of the terminal are restored.  The terminal is also
  given back when the program is stopped by a signal such as {c:HUP}, {c:INT}, {c:QUIT} or {c:TERM}.  A program
  that is killed with KILL cannot do it; then the command {c:reset} of the
  shell repairs the terminal.

.h2 Colors and text attributes [appterm-colour]
.ix Color, terminal
.ix Attributes, terminal
.ix Monochrome
.ix Reverse video
.ix Marker color
The terminal version paints its text in the colors that the Colors panel of
Setup sets ({n:str-colours}): blue with yellow text, until you change them.
It sets the background of every cell and clears the screen with the color of
normal text, so that the colors of your terminal do not show through, and it
puts the colors of the terminal back when it ends.  How many colors it can
use depends on the terminal.
.table 12 48
Terminal | What the program does
Any color | The terminal reports this in COLORTERM (truecolor or 24bit) or in
 | {c:TERM} (a name with truecolor or direct): every color is sent as it is.
256 colors | {c:TERM} has 256color in its name: a color that is not one of the
 | 256 is replaced by the nearest of them.
16 colors | The other terminals that have colors ({c:xterm}, {c:linux}, screen and the
 | like): the nearest of the 16 colors of the VGA palette.
No colors | {c:TERM} is not set, is dumb or unknown or starts with vt, or the
 | variable {c:STRATA_COLORS} is none or 0: the program sends the text
 | attributes that follow and no color but cyan.
.end
The variable {c:STRATA_COLORS} (none, 16, 256 or truecolor; 0 and 24bit are
the same as none and truecolor) settles the question when the terminal reports
it wrongly.  Any other value is ignored.  The colors of the terminal version
and
those of the X11 version are separate settings ({n:appcfg-key-colors}).
.table 17 43
Meaning | On a terminal that has no colors
Bold text | Text in bold, and the messages on the bottom row (which are also
  | in reverse video).
Dim | The dots for spaces and the signs for tabs and line ends, the filler of
  | the fields of a panel, the hints in a box, and menu entries that are
  | not available.
Italic text | Text in italics.
Underlined text | Text in underscored words.
Reverse video | The status line, the key labels, a menu, a chosen entry of a
  | list, a highlighted area, and a Keycap marker (its text).
Cyan | Markers in the text.
.end
On such a terminal a marker that is also in reverse video is a cyan
background.  If the terminal has no italic (or draws it as a color), the
text in italics is shown in whatever way the terminal does it, and nothing in
Strata changes; the status line
still says {c:Attr: Italic} when the cursor is in such text.  The same holds
for dim text.
.note The cursor is the cursor of the terminal, and Strata hides it while a
  menu or a box is open.  A terminal that cannot hide its cursor leaves it
  where the last cell was drawn.  Where the terminal has colors, the cursor
  color of the Colors panel (amber at first) is set through the terminal and
  the color of the terminal is restored at the end.

.h2 Characters [appterm-chars]
.ix Characters, terminal
.ix Box drawing characters
.ix Paste, terminal
The screen of Strata is a grid of cells that hold the characters of the set
of {n:appchar} (the Latin letters, the accented letters, the box drawing
characters, the arrows and the dots).  The terminal version draws the cells in
one of three ways, which are chosen when the program starts
({n:appterm-charset}).
.table 16 44
Screen | What is written
UTF-8 | Each cell as the Unicode character that has that shape: the double
  | and the single lines, the shaded blocks and the triangles.  The terminal
  | must read UTF-8, and its font must have the box drawing characters, the
  | dotted and shaded blocks and the two triangle arrows that mark the end of
  | a block.  A terminal that shows other shapes for them makes the boxes
  | and the guidelines look untidy and does nothing worse.
VT100 | Only ASCII characters are written.  The lines, the corners, the
  | shaded blocks, the arrows and the dots are drawn with the line characters
  | of the VT100 set, which the terminal itself provides: no locale, no UTF-8
  | and no special font are needed.  The set has single lines only, so the
  | double lines of a box become single lines.
ASCII | Plain characters that look like the shapes, such as {c:+}, {c:-} and
  | {c:|}.
.end
When the terminal cannot read UTF-8 at all, the program draws the VT100 screen
or, when the terminal does not speak VT100 either, the ASCII screen.  The text
of a document is never changed by the screen: a letter with an accent, or a
Greek letter, that the VT100 and ASCII screens cannot draw is shown as the
nearest ASCII letter, and the document keeps the letter.

Which characters can be typed, and what happens to the others, is in
{x:edit-special-chars} and {x:appchar-type}.  A text that you paste into the
terminal arrives as typed keys, one character after another: a character that
is not in the set is lost, a line end becomes {k:Enter} and so makes a new
block, and a tab is a tab.  The selection and the paste of the terminal work
as in any other program.
.note The UTF-8 screen needs a terminal in UTF-8 mode.  The screen is VT100
  unless the field Terminal Characters of Setup is changed ({n:appterm-charset}),
  and the choice Automatic checks the terminal at the start and draws the VT100
  screen, or the ASCII screen, when the terminal is not in UTF-8.

.h2 The start check and the three screens [appterm-charset]
.ix Start check, terminal
.ix ASCII characters
.ix VT100 characters
.ix Terminal Characters
.ix STRATA_ASCII
.ix UTF-8, check at start
.ix Font, terminal
The Editor and the Document Manager draw the screen of a terminal in one of
three ways: with UTF-8 characters, with the line characters of the VT100 set,
or with plain ASCII characters.  The default is the VT100 screen: when the field
Terminal Characters of Setup has not been changed, and has no value in the
settings, the screen is VT100, and nothing is asked of the terminal.  The
choices of the field are Automatic, ASCII, VT100 and UTF-8, in this order.  Only
with Automatic is the way chosen when the program starts.  Where the locale is
a UTF-8 locale, one box character is written in a corner of the screen, the
terminal is asked where the cursor is, and the answer is read before the first
key is read.
A terminal that reads UTF-8 moves the cursor by one column; a terminal that
reads Latin-1 or another set of eight bits shows three characters and moves it
by three.  The answer is never taken for typed keys, and neither is any other
answer that a terminal sends by itself.  Where the locale is not a UTF-8 locale
the question is not asked, because the answer could not change the choice, and
no character outside ASCII is written at all.

A terminal that is not in UTF-8 would show rubbish for the UTF-8 characters, so
Automatic draws the VT100 screen when the terminal can, and the ASCII screen
when it cannot, in the same cells, so that nothing moves.  A note on the
message line of the first screen reports this once, when Automatic made the
choice:
.code
No UTF-8 terminal: VT100 lines are drawn. See Setup, terminal_charset.
.end
or
.code
No UTF-8 or VT100 terminal: ASCII is drawn. See Setup, terminal_charset.
.end
The note goes with the first key, and the choice is also written in the log of
the run.  The choice follows this order.
.table 22 38
What | The result
The variable {c:STRATA_ASCII} | 1 means ASCII characters, 0 means UTF-8,
 | whatever the terminal reports.  Nothing is asked, and no message is shown.
The field Terminal Characters of Setup | ASCII, VT100 or UTF-8 is used as it
 | is, like the variable.  With no value in the settings the field is VT100,
 | the default.  Automatic goes on to the steps below.  Saved as
 | {c:terminal_charset} ({n:appcfg-key-charset}).
A terminal multiplexer (tmux, screen) | A UTF-8 locale means UTF-8.  Any other
 | locale means VT100 when the type of terminal ({c:TERM}) has the line
 | characters, and ASCII when it has not.  A multiplexer translates the
 | characters itself, so the answer of the terminal behind it is not used,
 | and nothing is asked.
A UTF-8 locale | The terminal is asked where its cursor is.  One column, or no
 | answer in 0.8 seconds, means UTF-8.  Any other number goes on.
Any other locale, or an answer of more than one column | VT100 when the
 | terminal can draw it, else ASCII.  The terminal can when its type has the
 | line characters in the terminal database, or when it answers the question
 | who it is (the device attributes) as a VT100 or a later model.  The
 | question is asked only when the type does not decide, and the wait is 0.1
 | second at most.
.end
Each of these steps leaves the start free to go on: a terminal that does not
answer never holds up the program for longer than the times named.  The
database of the terminal types is read from the usual directories of the system
({c:TERMINFO} and {c:TERMINFO_DIRS} are used when they are set).

What the VT100 screen draws:
.table 22 38
Shape | VT100
Horizontal and vertical lines | The lines of the set, single; double lines too
Corners, crossings, tees | The corners, crossing and tees of the set, single
Shaded blocks | The checkerboard of the set
Solid blocks and half blocks | A space in reverse video
Triangles and arrows | {c:>} {c:<} {c:^} {c:v}, as in ASCII
Dots and the bullet | The centered dot of the set
Diamond | The diamond of the set
Letters with an accent | The letter without the accent
Greek letters and math signs | The nearest letter or sign, as in ASCII
Any other shape | {c:?}
.end
What the ASCII screen draws:
.table 22 38
Shape | ASCII
Horizontal lines | {c:-}, and {c:=} for the double line
Vertical lines | {c:|}
Corners, crossings, tees | {c:+}
Shaded blocks | {c::} and {c:#}
Solid blocks and half blocks | {c:#}
Triangles and arrows | {c:<} {c:>} {c:^} {c:v}
Letters with an accent | The letter without the accent
Greek letters and math signs | The nearest letter or sign
Dots, the end of a block | {c:.} and {c:<}
Any other shape | {c:?}
.end
The text of your document is not changed: only the way that the terminal
draws it.  The keycaps are drawn as the key name in reverse video as always,
and need nothing else.  The X11 version draws its own shapes and is not
concerned.

The check cannot test the font.  A terminal that reads UTF-8 but has a font
without the shapes shows squares in place of the lines, the bars and the
triangle.  That is not a fault of the program, and the check cannot see it.
Two cures exist: give the terminal a font that has the shapes, or set the field
Terminal Characters of Setup to VT100 (the default: the lines are then drawn by
the terminal itself), or start with {c:STRATA_ASCII=1} in the environment.  A
terminal that reads Latin-1 and is told to read UTF-8 with {c:STRATA_ASCII=0}
or the field shows rubbish, and some of them type an answer to a question that
the program never asked; the program drops such answers, but do not force UTF-8
on a terminal that cannot read it.

To see what a terminal does, type these commands in it, outside Strata.  The
first prints the top of a box when the terminal speaks VT100:
.code
printf '\033(0lqqk\033(B\n'
.end
The second prints a horizontal line when the terminal reads UTF-8 and its font
has the shape:
.code
printf '\342\224\200\n'
.end
A terminal that is a VT100 shows {c:lqqk} drawn as a box top by the first
command, and a terminal that reads UTF-8 shows a line by the second.  When the
first shows the letters {c:lqqk}, set Terminal Characters to ASCII.

.h2 Screen sizes [appterm-size]
.ix Screen size, terminal
.ix 80 columns
.ix 25 rows
.ix Resize
.ix Small terminal
The terminal version uses the whole terminal, as many rows and as many columns
as the terminal reports.  If the terminal cannot give a size,
Strata uses 80 columns by 25 rows.  A change of the window is followed at
once, without a key: the screen is cleared, drawn again at the new size, and
the text is laid out again.
.table 15 45
Matter | What Strata does
Height that is kept | The program never changes the size of the window of
  | your terminal.  When you change the height of the terminal while the
  | Editor runs, the Editor keeps the number of rows in your settings at the end of
  | the run, and the next start uses at most that many rows.  Make the
  | terminal taller while the Editor runs to use more rows again.  The same
  | number is in Setup, in the field Window Rows, Terminal
  | ({n:str-setup-fields}); an empty field means all the rows of the
  | terminal, and so does a run in which you never changed the height.  The
  | number is 20 to 200, and the Editor always has at least 20 rows.
Width of the text | The text is laid out in at most 80 columns and at least
  | 40, whatever the width of the terminal. In a terminal
  | that is wider than 80 columns the text stays at the left
  | and the status line, the menu bar and the key labels use
  | the whole width.
Narrower than 40 | The text is still laid out for 40 columns and what does
  | not fit is cut off at the right edge of the screen.
  | Nothing wraps for the sake of the screen; use at least
  | 40 columns.
Number of rows | Two rows at the top, the status line and the ruler (the
  | menu bar and the entries of its menu cover them), two at
  | the bottom for the key labels when Display On-Screen
  | Help? is Yes, and the rest for the text.  The Editor
  | needs a terminal of at least 20 rows.  In a terminal
  | with fewer rows it still draws the screen, without an
  | error, but a box that does not fit is cut off.
Boxes | Menus, lists and panels are drawn in the middle of the
  | screen.  The Marker Commands box is 17 rows high and
  | Global Page Layout 16.  The Format panels are 12 or 13
  | rows high in a short terminal and spread over as many as
  | 23 rows in a tall one.  The Typeface List is the tallest:
  | about 24 rows (a row for each of the 20 fonts and one for
  | the printer, and the lines of the box).
.end
.caution A box that is taller than the screen is cut off at the top and at
  the bottom.  In a terminal of 15 rows the top and bottom lines of the
  Marker Commands box are not visible.  Use 24 rows or more: the panels of
  Strata, the Typeface List included, are seen in full on 80 by 24.

.h2 The Esc key and the Alt key [appterm-esc]
.ix Esc, terminal
.ix Alt, terminal
.ix Meta key
.ix Escape time
A terminal sends the cursor keys, the function keys and the keys with Alt
in a way that Strata has to read, and the terminal determines what is sent.
The keys that terminals of the {c:xterm} family send for the cursor keys,
{k:Home}, {k:End}, {k:Ins},
{k:Del}, {k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn}, {k:Shift-Tab} and {k:F1} to {k:F12}, with Shift,
Alt and Ctrl where the terminal sends them, are read.  A key that a terminal
sends in
another way is not seen: nothing happens, and no message is shown.

A terminal sends Alt and a key as {k:Esc} followed by the key, and sends
{k:Esc} itself alone.  The two are told apart by waiting: after an {k:Esc} the
Editor waits 40 thousandths of a second for the next key.  If none comes,
the key was {k:Esc}; if one comes, it is the second half of a key.
.table 20 40
Keys | Read as
{k:Esc} and nothing for 0.04 seconds | {k:Esc}.
{k:Esc} twice | {k:Esc}.
{k:Esc} and a letter, digit or sign | {k:Alt} with that character: {k:Alt-O},
  | {k:Alt-1}, {k:Alt-<}, {k:Alt-+}, and the like.
{k:Esc} and {k:Backspace} | {k:Alt-Backspace}.
{k:Esc} and {k:Enter} | {k:Alt-Enter}.
{k:Esc} and another control key | {k:Alt} and {k:Ctrl} with that letter.  A macro
  | can be defined on such a key; no command of Strata uses one.
{k:Esc} and the start of a cursor or function key | The key.  Each part of
  | it is waited for up to 0.05 seconds; if it does not come in time the key is
  | read as {k:Esc} and the rest is typed as characters.
.end
Three things follow.  First, the terminal must send {k:Esc} for Alt.  A
terminal that sets the eighth bit instead (the setting that is called "meta
sends eighth bit" or "Alt sends 8-bit" in some terminals) makes Strata type a
letter of the character set where you wanted a command.  The usual terminals
send {k:Esc} by default; {c:xterm} has a resource for it ({c:metaSendsEscape}).
Second, over a slow line or through a program that holds back what the
terminal sends, the parts of a key can arrive more than 0.04 or 0.05 seconds apart;
then a cursor key shows up as {k:Esc} followed by the characters {c:[} and
{c:A}.  A line that does this needs a remedy outside Strata
({n:appterm-links}).  Third, a real {k:Esc} is seen 0.04 seconds after it is
pressed, which nobody notices.

.h2 The control keys [appterm-ctrl]
.ix Control keys, terminal
.ix Backspace, terminal
.ix Hanging indent
Some control keys are sent by more than one key, and the keys cannot be told
apart.
.table 16 44
Key | What Strata does
{k:Ctrl-H} | Some terminals send it for {k:Backspace}, and then
  | {k:Backspace} sets a hanging indent.  Strata reads the Delete key as
  | {k:Backspace}: set the terminal to send Delete for {k:Backspace} (in
  | most it is the choice "ASCII DEL" or "Backspace sends delete").  Some
  | terminals send {k:Ctrl-H} for {k:Ctrl-Backspace}; use
  | {k:Alt-Backspace}.
{k:Ctrl-I}, {k:Tab} | A tab.  Italics is {k:Alt-I} in a terminal.
{k:Ctrl-J}, {k:Ctrl-M}, {k:Enter} | All end the line.  {k:Ctrl-Enter} is
  | {k:Enter}.
{k:Ctrl-[} | The same as {k:Esc}.
{k:Ctrl-^} | Edit Section or Edit Document.  Many keyboards need
  | {k:Ctrl-Shift-6} for it.  The same is in the Edit menu.
.end

.h2 Keys that a terminal keeps for itself [appterm-reach]
.ix Keys, alternates for terminals
.ix Alternates for terminals
.ix Ctrl-F menu
.ix Keypad keys, terminal
Many terminals, window managers and desktops keep some keys for themselves,
and some keys have no way to reach a terminal program.  Strata has an
alternate for each of the keys that this affects most; the alternates work in
the X11 version too.  {n:view} lists them ({n:view-terminal-keys}); this is
the full table.  A key that does nothing for a reason that is unknown is in
the document Errata ({n:errata-pgup}).
.table 16 18 25
Key | In a terminal | Use instead
{k:Alt-F2} to | Often kept by the | {k:Ctrl-F}: the Alt commands
 {k:Alt-F7}, | terminal or the | menu, one entry for each,
 {k:Alt-F9} | desktop. | chosen with {k:Enter}.
{k:Alt-F5} | The same. | {k:Alt-O}, or the entry of the
 (Overtype) |  | {k:Ctrl-F} menu.
{k:Alt-F12} | The same. | {k:Ctrl-F12}, or the {k:Ctrl-F}
 (Marker) |  | menu.
{k:Ctrl-I} | Is {k:Tab}. | {k:Alt-I}, or {k:Alt-F6}.
 (italics) |  |
{k:Ctrl-Backspace} | Is {k:Backspace}. | {k:Alt-Backspace}.
{k:Alt-[}, | {k:Esc} and [ start | {k:Alt-<}, {k:Alt->}, or
 {k:Alt-]} | a cursor key. | {k:Alt-,}, {k:Alt-.}.
{k:Keypad +}, | Send the plain | {k:Alt-+} (also {k:Alt-=}),
 {k:Keypad -}, | characters, which | {k:Alt--}, {k:Alt-*}.
 {k:Keypad *} | would be typed. |
{k:Keypad +} in a | The same. | {k:F2}.
 panel |  |
{k:Shift-Ins} | Some terminals use it | {k:Alt-F2} and Sub-section, or
  | to paste. | {k:Ctrl-F}, Append.
{k:Ctrl-^} | Needs | {k:F12}, Edit, Section or Document.
  | {k:Ctrl-Shift-6} on |
  | many keyboards. |
{k:Ctrl-Shift-Space} | Many terminals send | Both type the nonbreaking
 (nonbreaking | the same code as | space; see {n:edit-nbsp}.
 space) | {k:Ctrl-Space}. |
{k:Shift-Home}, | Not passed on by | {k:Alt-F7} (Go To): Document
 {k:Shift-End}, | many terminals. | start, Document end, Next
 {k:Shift-Up}, |  | section, Previous section.
 {k:Shift-Down} |  |
.end
The terminal and the desktop decide which keys reach Strata, and Strata does
not.  To test a key, press it in Strata.  If the status line and the message
line show nothing and the key does nothing, use the alternate or change the
setting of the terminal (most terminals can give up their own use of {k:F12},
{k:Alt-F4} and the like).

.h2 Which terminals keep which keys [appterm-keptby]
.ix Terminals, keys that they keep
.ix xterm, keys
.ix tmux, keys
The table of {x:appterm-reach} says what to use in place of a key.  This table
says where each key is lost: for each terminal and multiplexer that
was tried, the keys that it keeps and what to do about them.  The table is a
document of its own, kept apart from this manual
because it follows the terminals and not the program; the manual
includes it at this place with the Include marker, and the same document can
be opened or printed alone. 
.includedoc terminal-keys.doc

.h2 Links, multiplexers and slow lines [appterm-links]
.ix ssh
.ix tmux
.ix Slow line
.ix Remote login
Strata is built to be used over a remote login: only what changed is written,
and nothing is requested of the terminal that a text terminal does not have.  A
program that sits between the terminal and Strata (a multiplexer, a
serial line, a proxy) can change two things.
- It can take keys for itself, such as a prefix key, so that these keys never
  reach Strata.  Change the prefix of the multiplexer if it is a key that you
  need.
- It can change what a key sends, or hold back what follows {k:Esc}, so that a
  function key or a cursor key turns into {k:Esc} followed by letters.  Strata
  waits only 0.04 to 0.05 seconds after an {k:Esc} ({n:appterm-esc}); a multiplexer
  must be set to pass the keys with Shift, Ctrl and Alt as {c:xterm} sends them,
  or these keys are lost.
The program does not need the {c:TERM} of the terminal to be right.  It does
need the multiplexer to pass what the program writes.  A multiplexer that is
not in UTF-8 mode does not: tmux turns every character outside ASCII into an
underscore unless it is started with its UTF-8 option or the locale is a UTF-8
locale.  The Editor takes the locale as the
word of a multiplexer ({n:appterm-charset}), and draws ASCII characters when
it is not UTF-8.

.h2 What is different from X11 [appterm-diff]
.ix Terminal and X11, differences
.ix Differences between the versions
.table 16 21 22
Matter | Terminal | X11 ({n:appx11})
Window | The terminal. | One window of its own.
Size | The terminal's; changes | Fixed, 80 by 50 cells.
  | are followed. |
